# Lines of code

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-08-superposition/blob/4528c9d2dbe1550d2660dac903a8246076044905/pkg/sol/OwnershipNFTs.sol#L93


# Vulnerability details

## Overview

The OwnershipNFTs contract, which implements the ERC721 standard for NFTs, contains a faulty implementation in its _onTransferReceived function. The current implementation incorrectly rejects valid transfers, potentially preventing legitimate transfers to smart contract wallets or other contracts designed to receive ERC721 tokens.

Here is the faulty implementation:
```
 function _onTransferReceived(
        address _sender,
        address _from,
        address _to,
        uint256 _tokenId
    ) internal {
        // only call the callback if the receiver is a contract
        if (_to.code.length == 0) return;

        bytes4 data = IERC721TokenReceiver(_to).onERC721Received(
            _sender,
            _from,
            _tokenId,

            // this is empty byte data that can be optionally passed to
            // the contract we're confirming is able to receive NFTs
            ""
        );
         @//audit -- inverted assertion, should be ==
        require(
            data != IERC721TokenReceiver.onERC721Received.selector,
            "bad nft transfer received data"
        );
    }
```
As you can see, the inverted logic in the requirement check of the onERC721Received function's return value. The function currently reverts when it receives the correct selector, which is the opposite of the intended behavior.

Impact
The impact of this vulnerability is severe:

All safe transfers to contract addresses will fail, even if the receiving contract correctly implements the onERC721Received function.

## Proof of Concept
The test testIncorrectTransferRejection in the POC contract below shows that a transfer to a valid receiver (a contract correctly implementing onERC721Received) fails due to this bug.

here is the output logs:
```
