## Proof of Concept

The following is a step-by-step PoC to explain the issue in detail.

Let's assume a pool exists with the following config:

```python
token0 = X
token1 = FUSDC
tick_spacing = 3
```

The pool already has 2 positions:

```python
Position_1:
Tick_range = [0, 3]

Position_2:
Tick_range = [12, 15]

Current_price of the pool (divided by 2^96) lies somewhere between [0, 3].
```

Let's say a user A creates a position using the function:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-08-superposition/blob/main/pkg/sol/SeawaterAMM.sol#L346

```solidity
    function mintPositionBC5B086D(address /* token */, int32 /* lower */, int32 /* upper */) external returns (uint256 /* id */) {
        directDelegate(_getExecutorPosition());
    }
```

with the following params:

```python
pool = X
lower = 9
upper = 6
```

The call would be successful as there is no check that would validate whether:

```python
upper > lower
```

Now, the user adds liquidity Δ (> 0) in the created position. However, there would be a unusual set:

```python
ticks[6].liquidity_net = -Δ
ticks[9].liquidity_net = Δ
```

Now, let's say a user B tries to swap. He sets the ```price_limit``` somewhere between [12, 15]. This is to make sure the tick 6 gets crossed during the swap. Everything would go fine except when the tick 6 would get crossed:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-08-superposition/blob/main/pkg/seawater/src/pool.rs#L481

```rust
state.liquidity = liquidity_math::add_delta(state.liquidity, liquidity_net)?;
```

The calculation happens as:

```python
liquidity = liquidity - Δ
```

If Δ is sufficient enough to make the liquidity go negative, this would revert the swap transaction:

https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-08-superposition/blob/main/pkg/seawater/src/maths/liquidity_math.rs#L12

```rust
pub fn add_delta(x: u128, y: i128) -> Result<u128, Error> {
    if y < 0 {
        let z = x.overflowing_sub(-y as u128);

        if z.1 {
            Err(Error::LiquiditySub)
        } else {
            Ok(z.0)
        }
    } else {
        let z = x.overflowing_add(y as u128);
        if z.0 < x {
            Err(Error::LiquidityAdd)
        } else {
            Ok(z.0)
        }
    }
}
```

This would not let the swap happen, thus DoSing the transaction. 

The swap would have been successful if the invalid position was not there. Or, it was there with valid tick order:

```python
lower = 6
upper = 9
```

## Severity

The Readme of the contest repo contains - "We should follow Uniswap V3's math faithfully" - as one its main invariants. This means that this invariant must not be broken. This reports shows that this core invariant is broken.

The bad behavior described in this report is just one of the many bad behaviors that could happen. Thus, the issue is a High severity.

